# Running on a small host

The bundle leaves the API's .NET runtime on its defaults. On a host with little
memory you can opt in to the settings on this page. Each one trades something
away, so measure first and change one thing at a time.

## What the defaults do

ASP.NET Core apps use Server GC by default
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/memory)).
Since .NET 9, Server GC runs with DATAS turned on. DATAS starts with one heap,
adds and removes heaps as needed, and keeps the heap roughly in proportion to the app's
long-lived data
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/garbage-collection/datas)).

The runtime sets a default heap hard limit only when the process runs under a
memory limit, such as a container limit
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#heap-hard-limit)).
The bundle sets no container memory limit, so the API starts with no heap limit.

The runtime also turns on W^X by default. The default is `1` in .NET 10 on every
architecture except RISC-V
([dotnet/runtime `clrconfigvalues.h`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/60629d14374c56f1cb51819049ad1fa529307f8d/src/coreclr/inc/clrconfigvalues.h#L640-L644)).
With W^X on, the Linux runtime backs executable memory, such as JIT-compiled
code, with a memfd named `doublemapper`
([`doublemapping.cpp`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/60629d14374c56f1cb51819049ad1fa529307f8d/src/coreclr/minipal/Unix/doublemapping.cpp#L62))
and maps it `MAP_SHARED`, once for execution and again for writing
([`doublemapping.cpp`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/60629d14374c56f1cb51819049ad1fa529307f8d/src/coreclr/minipal/Unix/doublemapping.cpp#L196-L204),
[`GetRWMapping`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/60629d14374c56f1cb51819049ad1fa529307f8d/src/coreclr/minipal/Unix/doublemapping.cpp#L260-L264)).
The kernel backs memfd files with shmem
([`mm/memfd.c`](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/adc218676eef25575469234709c2d87185ca223a/mm/memfd.c#L393)),
and `/proc/<pid>/status` reports resident shmem as `RssShmem`
([kernel docs](https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/proc.html)). Microsoft's
[perfcollect guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/trace-perfcollect-lttng)
shows the same mapping by name, `memfd:doublemapper`.

## Measure first

Sample the API's runtime counters from inside the container:

```bash
case "$(uname -m)" in aarch64|arm64) rid=linux-arm64 ;; *) rid=linux-x64 ;; esac
curl -L -o /tmp/dotnet-counters https://aka.ms/dotnet-counters/$rid
chmod +x /tmp/dotnet-counters
docker compose cp /tmp/dotnet-counters nocturne-api:/tmp/dotnet-counters
docker compose exec nocturne-api /tmp/dotnet-counters collect -p 1 --counters System.Runtime \
  --duration 00:00:00:20 --format csv -o /tmp/counters.csv
docker compose exec nocturne-api cat /tmp/counters.csv | grep -E 'committed_size|heap.size|working_set'
docker compose exec nocturne-api cat /proc/1/status | grep -E 'VmRSS|RssAnon|RssShmem'
```

`dotnet.gc.last_collection.memory.committed_size` is the memory the GC has
committed. It can be larger than `dotnet.gc.last_collection.heap.size` because it
also holds memory ready for new allocations
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/built-in-metrics-runtime)).
`VmRSS` is the sum of `RssAnon`, `RssFile` and `RssShmem`
([kernel docs](https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/proc.html)).

## Opt-in settings

Put the settings in a `docker-compose.override.yaml` next to
`docker-compose.yaml`. When you run `docker compose up -d` with no `-f`, Compose
loads that file on top of the base file
([compose-go](https://github.com/compose-spec/compose-go/blob/a1350eaeeb0e74c2e7fb52acf7d567625b01bd47/cli/options.go#L484-L488)).
Once you pass `-f`, for example for the byo-proxy override, Compose reads only the
files you name, so add `-f docker-compose.override.yaml` as well
([Docker docs](https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/multiple-compose-files/merge/),
[compose-go](https://github.com/compose-spec/compose-go/blob/a1350eaeeb0e74c2e7fb52acf7d567625b01bd47/cli/options.go#L214-L216)).

```yaml
services:
  nocturne-api:
    environment:
      # Hex bytes. 0x18000000 is 384 MiB.
      DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit: "0x18000000"
      DOTNET_GCConserveMemory: "5"
      DOTNET_EnableWriteXorExecute: "0"
```

### `DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit`

Sets the most memory the GC heap and its bookkeeping may commit. Environment
variables take the value in hex
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#heap-hard-limit)).
An allocation that would push the committed heap past the limit, even after a
full compacting GC, throws `OutOfMemoryException`
([dotnet/designs](https://github.com/dotnet/designs/blob/59f0fdd899978d3f0e9f5660ee3feb434ed21147/accepted/2019/support-for-memory-limits.md?plain=1#L51)).
If large imports start failing, raise it.

Setting `mem_limit` on `nocturne-api` instead also caps the heap. Under a memory
limit the default heap hard limit is 75% of that limit, or 20 MB if that is
larger ([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#heap-hard-limit-percent)).
The limit then covers the whole container, not only the heap, and by default the
kernel kills processes in a container that runs out of memory
([Docker docs](https://docs.docker.com/engine/containers/resource_constraints/)).

### `DOTNET_GCConserveMemory`

Takes `0` to `9`. Higher values make the GC work harder to keep the heap small,
at the cost of more frequent collections and possibly longer pauses. Any non-zero
value also makes the GC compact the large object heap automatically if it has too
much fragmentation
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#conserve-memory)).
DATAS already uses `5` in its gen0 budget formula when this setting is unset
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#gen0-max-budget-settings)),
so `5` keeps that budget and adds the large object heap compaction. Values above
`5` shrink the budget further.

### `DOTNET_EnableWriteXorExecute`

`0` turns off W^X, so the runtime stops double mapping executable memory.

<Callout type="warning" title="This turns off a security hardening">

  With W^X on, no page of memory is writable and executable at the same time.
  Microsoft recommends leaving it on where startup time is not critical
  (<a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet/announcing-net-6-preview-7/">.NET 6 Preview 7 announcement</a>).
  None of the sources on this page give a figure for how much memory W^X costs.
  Compare <code>VmRSS</code> before and after, and turn it back on if the saving
  is small.

</Callout>

### Workstation GC

`DOTNET_gcServer=0` switches the API to Workstation GC. Microsoft's ASP.NET Core
memory guide says Workstation GC might perform better when several containerized
apps share a machine
([Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/memory)),
but its comparison does not mention DATAS, which already starts Server GC with one
heap. This page leaves it out for that reason. If you try it, measure before and
after.
